From 9d0e50e4f3719c1e9b801356d4f0238e0de22c61 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: iasnezhkov <iasnezhkov@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 15:16:43 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] v4l2: encode camera surface YV12 natively, drop the I420 hack

Camera recording through the AIDL GraphicBufferSource input surface
(debug.stagefright.c2inputsurface=-1) feeds c2.v4l2.avc/hevc.encoder
with MTK gralloc buffers allocated for HAL_PIXEL_FORMAT_IMPLEMENTATION_
DEFINED + GRALLOC_USAGE_HW_VIDEO_ENCODER, which MTK gralloc resolves to
YV12 at 2560x1440 (device log: "[checkPlaneSize] [YV12:2] sizeInBytes:
925440 != 1280 x 720"). The encoder always failed to queue that frame:
"V4L2Encoder: Failed to queue input buffer using QueueDMABuf", "Failed
to enqueue input frame", then "EncodeComponent: Trying to queue work
item while component is not running" once the component tripped into
ERROR.

Root cause was in EncodeComponent::getVideoFrameLayout(). For a
Y/Cr/Cb-ordered planar buffer (crcb == true, !semiplanar, i.e. real
YV12) it forced the reported VideoPixelFormat to I420 ("HACK: pretend
YV12 is I420 now since VEA only accepts I420", a leftover from the old
VideoEncodeAccelerator wrapper this HAL no longer uses). The plane
offsets/strides it returns are already in physical memory order (Y,
then whichever chroma plane comes second, then the last one), so for
a real YV12 buffer that vector is [Y, V, U] mislabeled as I420's
[Y, U, V] - swapped chroma. V4L2Encoder was also always initialized
with a fixed NV12 OUTPUT format (VideoEncoder::kInputPixelFormat) and
never looked at the buffer's actual layout again, so
EncodeComponent::queueTask() only ever had one way to bridge a format
mismatch: spin up a CPU FormatConverter. mtk-vcodec-enc.c's OUTPUT
queue_setup/buf_prepare already natively accepts YUV420(M), YVU420(M),
NV12(M) and NV21(M) (see queue_setup: "if (*nplanes != q_data->fmt->
num_planes) return -EINVAL", vb2ops_venc_buf_prepare same check), so
none of that conversion is needed for YV12/NV12/NV21 camera buffers.

Fix:
- getVideoFrameLayout() now reports VideoPixelFormat::YV12 (not I420)
  for a real Y/V/U planar buffer, matching the plane order it already
  returns.
- VideoEncoder/V4L2Encoder gain reconfigureInputFormat(format, planes).
  On the first real frame, if it doesn't match the V4L2 device's
  current (NV12-default) input format, EncodeComponent now first tries
  V4L2Encoder::reconfigureInputFormat() before falling back to the CPU
  FormatConverter. It destroys the input buffers, re-runs
  configureInputFormat() with the requested format and the buffer's
  own plane[0] stride (so the multi-planar Fourcc - YM21/NM12/NM21 -
  is tried first, matching mtk-vcodec-enc.c's per-plane bytesperline
  path), and only accepts the result if the negotiated format, plane
  count and Y stride all match what the buffer actually has;
  mtk-vcodec-enc.c's vidioc_try_fmt() recomputes OUTPUT bytesperline
  from the aligned width and ignores what userspace asked for, so this
  check is what catches a silent stride mismatch instead of shipping
  a torn/skewed frame. On any mismatch it reverts to the previous
  format and buffer count and returns false so the existing CPU
  conversion path still runs, e.g. for RGB IMPLEMENTATION_DEFINED
  buffers this driver cannot take directly.
- enqueueInputBuffer() duplicates the frame's single dma-buf fd across
  all V4L2 memory planes when a multi-planar format (YVU420M etc.) is
  used with a single-fd gralloc buffer. V4L2WritableBufferRef::
  queueDMABuf() indexes fds[0..numPlanes) unconditionally
  (V4L2Device.cpp) with no bounds check, so a 3-plane format fed a
  1-fd vector (the normal case for both the camera's YV12 buffer and
  most gralloc NV12 buffers, which are one allocation with internal
  plane offsets, not one dma-buf per plane) was an out-of-bounds
  vector read producing a garbage fd for planes 1/2 - independently
  enough to make QueueDMABuf fail with EBADF on its own.
- V4L2Device::v4L2ProfileToC2Profile() was missing
  V4L2_MPEG_VIDEO_H264_PROFILE_HIGH_10 (value 5) in its H.264 switch,
  logging "Unknown profile: 5" whenever the driver's H264_PROFILE
  control menu includes it; c2ProfileToV4L2H264Profile() already maps
  PROFILE_AVC_HIGH_10 back to it, so this was a one-sided gap in the
  reverse direction. Added the missing case.

Verified so far: static read-through against mtk-vcodec-enc.c's
vidioc_try_fmt/vb2ops_venc_queue_setup/vb2ops_venc_buf_prepare
(kernel612-common, mindone/modules/mtk_vcodec_enc_v1) and against
V4L2Device.cpp's Fourcc/plane-layout helpers to confirm YM21/NM12/NM21
negotiate the multi-planar path these buffers need and that the
existing offset/stride plumbing (EncodeHelpers.cpp
getGraphicBlockInfo(), already Y-relative) needed no changes. Not yet
run on-device. To verify: record video through the camera app (input
surface path) at 2560x1440 and confirm no "Failed to queue input
buffer using QueueDMABuf" / "Failed to enqueue input frame" /
"Trying to queue work item while component is not running"; expect
one "Reconfiguring V4L2 input format from NV12 to YV12" followed by
"Reconfigured V4L2 input format to YV12, no conversion needed" instead
of "Creating input format convertor"; confirm the recorded video has
correct colors (would show as a green/purple chroma swap if the
plane-order assumption were wrong) and no tearing (would show as
diagonal chroma smear if the stride check above were wrong); and
capture-preview/non-surface encode paths (already NV12) are unaffected.

Device run (25.09) found a second, independent bug past the one above:
reconfiguration itself worked exactly as designed - "Reconfiguring V4L2
input format from NV12 to YV12 to match the actual buffer layout", the
driver accepted it (vidioc_try_fmt w/h (2560,1440) sizeimage[3686400,
921600,921600], 3 planes allocated) - but then "(OUTPUT_MPLANE)VIDIOC_QBUF
failed", and with videobuf2 debug on the kernel side gave the real reason:
"__prepare_dmabuf: invalid dmabuf fd for plane 0" / "__buf_prepare: buffer
preparation failed: -22". createInputFrame() (this file) read
block.handle()->data[0..numFds) unconditionally, assuming every
C2ConstGraphicBlock is backed by a plain C2Handle/native_handle_t whose
data[] entries are dma-buf fds. On this Android 16 build, GraphicBufferSource
hands the input surface's frames to the Codec2 client as IGBA/AHardwareBuffer
-backed blocks (_C2BlockPoolData::TYPE_AHWBUFFER), whose C2Handle is an
opaque IGBA handle, not a native_handle_t of fds - block.handle()->data[0]
was some other integer, so a garbage value went to the kernel as a dma-buf
fd. The camera's own MTK gralloc YV12 buffer underneath is unaffected by
this - the pixel data and padded (925440-byte) chroma planes are exactly
what the format-detection and stride-match work above already handles;
only the fd this file extracted from the wrapping C2 block was wrong.

Fixed by adding getInputBlockFds(), which asks
_C2BlockFactory::GetGraphicBlockPoolData(block) (codec2_vndk's own
internal accessor, already reachable here through the libcodec2_internal
header_libs dependency this target already has) for the block's pool
data; if its getType() is TYPE_AHWBUFFER, it gets the real AHardwareBuffer
via _C2BlockFactory::GetAHardwareBuffer() (the same VNDK helper this
repo's own VideoFramePool.cpp already uses for its IGBA case) and reads
the dma-buf fd(s) from AHardwareBuffer_getNativeHandle()'s native_handle_t
instead - a plain, already-imported native_handle_t with the identical
numFds/data[] fd convention, so the existing per-plane loop is reused
unchanged. Anything not TYPE_AHWBUFFER (a plain gralloc/bufferqueue block,
the pre-existing case) still goes through block.handle() as before. No fd
is duplicated: the fetched AHardwareBuffer's native handle is owned by the
block's own pool data for as long as the C2ConstGraphicBlock is alive
(_C2BlockFactory::GetGraphicBlockPoolData() returns the block's own
poolData() member, not a newly constructed object), and the block itself
is kept alive by the C2Work sitting in EncodeComponent::mWorkQueue until
onInputBufferDone() releases it - the same lifetime window the
pre-existing, never-duplicated gralloc-fd path already relied on. Needed
no Android.bp change: components/Android.bp already lists
libcodec2_internal in header_libs (for C2BlockInternal.h) and
libnativewindow in shared_libs (for vndk/hardware_buffer.h's
AHardwareBuffer_getNativeHandle), both confirmed against the real
Android.bp in this tree.

Verified on device (25.09): the format/stride-reconfiguration logic above
is now confirmed correct against a real MTK gralloc YV12 camera buffer at
2560x1440 (driver-side vidioc_try_fmt log matches the requested layout, 3
planes negotiated) - only the fd-extraction bug fixed in this amendment
blocked it from queueing. Not yet re-run after this fix. To verify: repeat
the same 2560x1440 camera-surface recording; expect no "invalid dmabuf fd
for plane" / "VIDIOC_QBUF failed" / buffer preparation failed: -22 from
the kernel, a clean encoded stream, correct colors and no chroma tearing
(both already called out above); and that the plain-gralloc (non-surface,
byte-buffer) encode path, which never carries an AHWBUFFER-backed block
and so never hit this bug, is unaffected.

Device run (25.09, evening) showed the AHWBUFFER branch was not the case
that mattered: the camera and screenrecord frames reach the encoder as
plain gralloc blocks (no pool data), and a temporary log of their handle
gave numFds=3, numInts=85 with data[0] -> anon_inode:gralloc_extra (MTK
gralloc metadata), data[1] -> /dmabuf:2560x1440-YV12-halbuf (the buffer)
and data[2] -> an ashmem region. The kernel rejected data[0]
("__prepare_dmabuf: invalid dmabuf fd for plane 0"). getInputBlockFds()
now keeps only the fds that are dma-bufs, recognised the way
libdmabufinfo does it, by the /proc/self/fd link starting with
"/dmabuf". fstatfs() with DMA_BUF_MAGIC was tried first, but the c2
service domain has no filesystem getattr on the dmabuf and anon_inodefs
superblocks (avc denied, unlabeled) and granting that would be wrong,
while reading the links of its own fds needs nothing new.

Verified on the device (2509-K5, ROM 0925c, this service bind-mounted):
CamRig video 2560x1440 AVC 24 Mbit/s for 10 s -> "Reconfiguring V4L2
input format from NV12 to YV12", no QBUF error, 290 frames in 9.79 s =
29.52 fps, largest gap 66.7 ms, 23.7 Mbit/s, 29 MB MP4 (ffprobe: h264
2560x1440 yuv420p, 290 frames); an extracted frame shows correct colours
and no chroma skew.

